/* book Worked example: Q: A 1024-address bit-oriented SRAM is tested with March C−. Count the operations per address and the total accesses. At 5 ns per access, find the test time. Compare it with GALPAT at about 4n² accesses.
   1. Operations per address: ⇕(w0) 1, ⇑(r0, w1) 2, ⇑(r1, w0) 2, ⇓(r0, w1) 2, ⇓(r1, w0) 2, ⇕(r0) 1. Sum k = 10.
   2. Total accesses = 10 × 1024 = 10,240. Test time = 10,240 × 5 ns = 51,200 ns = 51.2 µs.
   3. GALPAT: 4 × 1024² = 4 × 1,048,576 = 4,194,304 accesses. Time = 4,194,304 × 5 ns = 20.97 ms.
   4. Ratio = 4,194,304 ÷ 10,240 = 409.6.
   Answer: March C− needs 10,240 accesses and 51.2 µs; GALPAT needs 4,194,304 accesses and about 21 ms, 409.6 times longer, so March C− is the production choice. */
