/* book Worked example 2: Q: An SoC has a TAM 8 wires wide. Core A has 4 internal scan chains of 200 cells and 500 patterns; core B has 4 chains of 150 cells and 800 patterns. Neither core can use more than 4 TAM wires. Find the total test time (a) testing A and then B, each given all 8 wires, and (b) testing both together on 4 wires each. The scan clock is 100 MHz.
   1. Core A: T = (200 + 1) × 500 + 200 = 100,500 + 200 = 100,700 clocks.
   2. Core B: T = (150 + 1) × 800 + 150 = 120,800 + 150 = 120,950 clocks.
   3. (a) One after the other: 8 wires do not help, since each core has only 4 chains. Total = 100,700 + 120,950 = 221,650 clocks = 2.217 ms.
   4. (b) Together on 4 + 4 wires: total = the longer core = 120,950 clocks = 1.210 ms.
   5. Saving = 221,650 − 120,950 = 100,700 clocks, 45.4 %. If the cores could use the full width, the serial plan would be as fast; sharing pays here because each core uses only half the TAM.
   Answer: 2.217 ms when the cores are tested one after the other and 1.210 ms when they share the TAM 4 + 4, a 45.4 % saving, as long as the power limit allows both cores to run together. */
