/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A digital line has two values and a stuck-at model, so ATPG can aim at one fault (6.3).
   - A hard (catastrophic) fault is an open, a short or a dead component.
   - Quick structural checks find hard faults cheaply.
   - For an N-bit ADC with full-scale range FSR, 1 LSB = FSR ÷ 2N.
   - DNLk = Wk ÷ (1 LSB) − 1
   - where DNLk is the differential nonlinearity of code k and Wk the measured width of that code in volts.
   - INLk = DNL1 + DNL2 + … + DNLk
   - where INLk is the integral nonlinearity at the upper edge of code k, its distance from the ideal straight line in LSB; the INL quoted on a data sheet is the largest magnitude.
   - SNR is signal power over noise power, with the harmonics left out; SINAD is signal power over noise plus distortion.
   - The servo method finds each code edge in turn with a feedback loop: accurate but slow.
*/
