/* book Worked example: Q: An 8-bit ADC has a full-scale range of 2.56 V. A ramp histogram gives code widths W64 = 14 mV and W65 = 6 mV, and INL63 = 0 LSB. (a) Find 1 LSB. (b) Find DNL at codes 64 and 65. (c) Find INL at codes 64 and 65. (d) A third code has a measured width of 0 mV; give its DNL and what it means.
   1. (a) 2⁸ = 256 codes, so 1 LSB = 2.56 V ÷ 256 = 0.010 V = 10 mV.
   2. (b) DNL64 = 14 ÷ 10 − 1 = +0.4 LSB. DNL65 = 6 ÷ 10 − 1 = −0.4 LSB.
   3. (c) INL64 = INL63 + DNL64 = 0 + 0.4 = +0.4 LSB. INL65 = 0.4 + (−0.4) = 0.0 LSB.
   4. (d) DNL = 0 ÷ 10 − 1 = −1 LSB: the code never appears, a missing code. At that code INL drops by 1 LSB from the previous code's value; it looks near zero only if the previous INL was about +1 LSB.
   5. Check: the two code widths add to 20 mV = 2 LSB, the same as two ideal codes, which is why INL returns to 0 after code 65.
   Answer: 1 LSB = 10 mV; DNL64 = +0.4 LSB and DNL65 = −0.4 LSB; INL64 = +0.4 LSB and INL65 = 0.0 LSB; a 0 mV code has DNL = −1 LSB and is missing. */
