/* book exam traps: Treating DNL and INL as the same. DNL is the error of one code width; INL is the running sum of DNL, the distance from the ideal line. | Writing a missing code as INL = −1 LSB. A missing code has DNL = −1 LSB; INL at that code falls by 1 LSB from its previous value. | Finding INL before removing offset and gain error. A tilted transfer curve is gain error, not a large INL. | Dividing by 2N − 1 for 1 LSB when the question defines FSR over 2N codes. Use FSR ÷ 2N unless told otherwise. | Calling SINAD 'SNR'. SNR leaves the harmonics out; SINAD includes them, and ENOB is computed from SINAD. */
